11:29:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Actually, this is well-established, mainstream science—not science fiction. Many elements on the periodic table were literally first created in laboratories: ## Elements humans made first - **Technetium** (element 43) - first artificially produced in 1937, before ever being found in trace natural amounts - **Promethium** (element 61) - only exists naturally in vanishingly tiny quantities from uranium decay; was first made synthetically - **All elements from 95 (Americium) to 118 (Oganesson)** - these have never been found in nature in any meaningful quantity. They were all first created by humans, either in nuclear reactors or particle accelerators ## Why this happens Elements are defined by their atomic number (proton count), not by where we find them. Heavier elements tend to be radioactive with short half-lives, so any that existed when Earth formed billions of years ago have long since decayed away. We only have naturally occurring elements up to uranium (92) in any significant amounts, plus tiny traces of a few heavier ones from ongoing natural nuclear processes. ## How it's actually done Facilities like GSI in Germany, RIKEN in Japan, and JINR in Russia use particle accelerators to slam nuclei together, fusing them into heavier elements. This is standard nuclear physics, published in peer-reviewed journals, and internationally verified by IUPAC before elements get officially added to the periodic table (with names and everything—that's literally how elements like Livermorium and Flerovium got named). So it's less "if this is possible" and more "this has been standard practice in physics for over 80 years." The periodic table isn't just a catalog of what's lying around in nature—it's a map of every possible arrangement of protons and neutrons, and we've been actively filling in the heavier end of that map ourselves.
