11:30:09 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're raising a genuinely interesting philosophical question, and there's real substance to it—but here's the scientific counterargument: ## The case for "it counts" **Existence isn't about duration**: An atom is defined by having a specific number of protons in its nucleus. If a nucleus with 118 protons exists for even a nanosecond, held together by the same nuclear forces as any other atom, it's just as "real" during that time as an atom of oxygen that exists for billions of years. Its existence isn't diminished by brevity—it did occupy space, have mass, have electrons (briefly) orbiting it, etc. **We can still measure real properties**: Even in milliseconds, scientists can detect decay signatures, measure approximate half-lives, and infer chemical behavior (some superheavy elements have been tested for volatility and reactivity using rapid chemical techniques before they decay). So we're not just seeing "a flash"—we're gathering actual data about a physical object's real behavior. **Instability isn't unique to synthetic elements**: Many naturally occurring isotopes are radioactive and decay in fractions of a second too—like some isotopes of astatine or francium, which occur in trace amounts on Earth right now. We don't say those "don't count" as real elements just because individual atoms are short-lived. ## Where your skepticism has a point - These fleeting existences make them scientifically **fascinating but practically useless**—you can't build anything from Oganesson, and it will likely never have any application. - There's a legitimate philosophical debate about whether something that can never be observed directly (only inferred from decay products) has the same ontological status as something you can hold in your hand. - If none of an element's isotopes could ever be stable even in principle, some might argue it's more of a mathematical possibility we're proving true, rather than something we're "making" in the everyday sense of the word. So: chemically and physically, by the standard definitions used in science, yes—these are real elements. But your instinct that this stretches the everyday meaning of "making something" is fair too. It's less "manufacturing a substance" and more "briefly proving a configuration of matter can exist."
